    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chemical defense is a complex trait by which organisms carry toxic or repellant compounds to discourage predation. South American poison frogs (Family Dendrobatidae) are particularly famous chemically defended organisms. Previous research has shown that these frogs take up potent organic alkaloid toxins from their diet and sequester them to be released when threatened – all without intoxicating themselves. The mechanism by which poison frogs resist self-intoxication is not fully understood. One way of dealing with ingested toxins is compound sequestration via circulating toxin-binding proteins that soak up excess alkaloids and protect vulnerable tissues. One such protein, saxiphilin, is thought to play a similar role in bullfrogs. This project examines saxiphilin: a blood-bound toxin-binding protein found in multiple species of poison frogs. My thesis aims to elucidate the role of saxiphilin as a proposed mechanism of toxin resistance in poison frogs. I first assessed how saxiphilin varies genetically and structurally between poison frog species with different toxin-carrying abilities or “toxin profiles,” analyzing expression patterns across tissues, and computationally predicting the binding activity of saxiphilin between species. Saxiphilin sequence is variable within and across poison frog species, but is largely conserved at regions corresponding to toxin-binding sites on the protein. Moreover, computational models of saxiphilin predict differential binding activity between saxiphilin derived from the bullfrog Rana catesbeiana and saxiphilin derived from poison frogs. Lastly, qPCR results demonstrate that expression of saxiphilin varies between tissues, with highest expression in the liver, brain, and eyes. These results support previous hypotheses that saxiphilin could act as a protective protein, conferring toxin resistance by binding to and neutralizing toxins in the frogs’ bodies. Overall, this research expands our understanding of poison frog toxicity, and addresses a long-standing question about poison frog physiology and biochemistry.",
